From 9336dcbe8573a89a4abbc73a47107be951f2014b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ElXreno <elxreno@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 16 Aug 2026 04:55:24 +0300
Subject: [PATCH] spl: stop grouping the slab caches with the data they
 describe

SLAB_RECLAIM_ACCOUNT asks the page allocator for reclaimable pageblocks,
which is where scatter abd chunks already go. A chunk is reclaimable in
the sense the allocator means: its owner hands it back on request. A slab
page is not, it comes back only once its last object dies, and for these
caches that is rare. Sharing pageblocks between the two leaves every block
holding a live object of any of them, and none of them come free.

Measured in a vm, arc holding 1GiB of data after a shrink: pageblocks with
more than one immovable owner fall from 178 to 85 and from 135 to 72 over
two runs, and the blocks holding free memory out of reach fall with them.

Nothing is lost in reclaim: the flag never made the kernel free anything,
the shrinker this module registers does that and runs either way. What is
lost is the MemAvailable estimate, which counts about half of this memory,
340MiB out of 62GiB here.
---
 module/os/linux/spl/spl-kmem-cache.c | 8 ++++++--
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/module/os/linux/spl/spl-kmem-cache.c b/module/os/linux/spl/spl-kmem-cache.c
index 6d496e6..cea22f0 100644
--- a/module/os/linux/spl/spl-kmem-cache.c
+++ b/module/os/linux/spl/spl-kmem-cache.c
@@ -781,8 +781,12 @@ spl_kmem_cache_create(const char *name, size_t size, size_t align,
 		if (size > spl_kmem_cache_slab_limit)
 			goto out;
 
-		if (skc->skc_flags & KMC_RECLAIMABLE)
-			slabflags |= SLAB_RECLAIM_ACCOUNT;
+		/*
+		 * KMC_RECLAIMABLE deliberately does not become
+		 * SLAB_RECLAIM_ACCOUNT: that would put these caches in the
+		 * pageblocks the abd chunks use, and a block holding both never
+		 * comes free. It never made the kernel reclaim anything anyway.
+		 */
 
 		skc->skc_linux_cache = kmem_cache_create_usercopy(
 		    skc->skc_name, size, align, slabflags, 0, size, NULL);
-- 
2.55.0

